---
import { css } from 'styled-system/css'
import Docs from '../../../layouts/Docs.astro'
import Callout from 'web-design-system/astro/marketing/Callout.astro'
import { docGuides, guideLinks, REPO_URL } from '../../../data/docs'

const toc = [
  { label: 'What lives where', href: '#layout' },
  { label: 'Runtime shape', href: '#runtime' },
  { label: 'Current status', href: '#status' },
  { label: 'Reading order', href: '#reading-order' },
  { label: 'Ground rules', href: '#rules' },
]

const guide = docGuides.find((entry) => entry.heading === 'Contributor guide')
const pages = guide ? guideLinks(guide).filter((item) => item.href !== guide.href) : []

const summaries: Record<string, string> = {
  'Adapter contracts': 'The tier model, the UI-free harness trait, events, capabilities, approvals and contract tests.',
  'Annotation API': 'The shared payload, wrapper security model, validation bounds and prompt assembly.',
  'Quota ledger': 'The planned local record for pool slots, reset windows, cooldowns and quota-aware selection.',
  'Release pipeline': 'Packaging checks, smoke workflows, the clean-machine gate, and what signing still requires.',
  'Test fixtures': 'Recorded protocol fixtures, fake adapters, Playwright surfaces and gated live smokes.',
}

const layout = [
  { path: 'libs/shepherd-core', owns: 'Agents, terminals, task state, Git, storage, input routing and commands. No Tauri or GPUI types.' },
  { path: 'libs/agent-harness', owns: 'The UI-free adapter trait, transcript event types and native adapter implementations.' },
  { path: 'libs/acp-client', owns: 'ACP connection, sessions, permissions, filesystem and terminal client capabilities.' },
  { path: 'apps/shepherd/ui', owns: 'SolidJS views over the core bridge: task workspace, canvas terminal, preview and review.' },
  { path: 'apps/shepherd/src-tauri', owns: 'The window, Tauri commands, the event channel and the loopback preview wrapper.' },
  { path: 'apps/shepherd-gpui', owns: 'The planned v2 shell over the same core; it is not part of the v1 path.' },
]
---

<Docs
  title="Contributor guide"
  description="How Shepherd is put together, where each responsibility lives, and how to work on the core without breaking the UI contract."
  toc={toc}
>
  <Callout tone="note" title="Here to use the app?">
    This guide is about the implementation. Installing, running and configuring Shepherd is the
    <a href="/docs/users">user guide</a>.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="layout">What lives where</h2>
  <p>
    Shepherd is one application with a UI-free Rust core. The v1 shell is Tauri 2 with a SolidJS and
    TypeScript frontend; v2 is planned to replace only the shell with GPUI. The core must stay
    reusable across both.
  </p>
  <ul>
    {
      layout.map((entry) => (
        <li><code>{entry.path}</code> — {entry.owns}</li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2 id="runtime">Runtime shape</h2>
  <p>
    One Rust process owns the tokio runtime, every agent process, every PTY, the Git commands and
    the database writes. The shell sends commands and receives core events over one channel. The UI
    renders core snapshots; it is not a second frontend model.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no daemon and no attach protocol. The only listener is the token-scoped loopback preview
    wrapper, and it exists only while the Preview tab is using it. Quitting the app ends agents and
    terminals by design; the recovery path is the interrupted state, not a background service.
  </p>

  <h2 id="status">Current status</h2>
  <p>
    The v1 workspace is functional: native and ACP adapters, task worktrees, review, Git, terminals,
    preview annotation and the settings/shell flows are implemented and exercised. The quota-aware
    agent pool and signed installers are next, not shipped. The v2 GPUI shell is planned but
    unscheduled and starts only after v1 is feature complete.
  </p>
  <p>
    When a task and this guide disagree, the task in <code>apps/shepherd/docs/todo.md</code> and the
    master specification are the source of truth. If a planned feature is documented here, the page
    says so.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reading-order">Reading order</h2>
  <ol>
    {
      pages.map((page) => (
        <li>
          <a href={page.href}><strong>{page.label}</strong></a> — {summaries[page.label]}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>
  <p>
    The behavior lives in <code>apps/shepherd/docs/spec/master-spec.md</code>, the workspace layout
    decisions in <code>workspace-ui.md</code>, and the build shape in <code>stack.md</code>. Read
    the relevant spec before changing a flow that the spec covers.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rules">Ground rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Local-first.</strong> No account, telemetry, crash upload or provider key. Agents use the credentials you already installed.</li>
    <li><strong>No silent allow.</strong> An approval without a judge becomes a user card; it never becomes an automatic yes.</li>
    <li><strong>No hidden state.</strong> The task shows the adapter tier, spawn command, fallback reason and every decision source.</li>
    <li><strong>Adapters do not write storage.</strong> The core owns turn boundaries, canonical item IDs and persistence.</li>
    <li><strong>Errors reach the UI.</strong> Do not discard a fallible operation with <code>let _ =</code>; propagate it or surface it with context.</li>
    <li><strong>Tests assert behavior, not pixels.</strong> Terminal, Git, adapter and preview tests use fixtures and temporary repositories.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class={css({ fontSize: 'sm', color: 'fg.subtle' })}>
    Repository: <a href={REPO_URL}>{REPO_URL.replace('https://', '')}</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
